+# Schema-Driven Type Safety
+
+## Problem Statement
+Previously, SQL strings in `src/db/` were untyped — TypeScript couldn't validate them. When a migration renamed a column (e.g., `created_at` → `created_on`), the code didn't fail until query execution on real data.
+
+## Solution: Drizzle ORM with Schema-as-Code
+
+Schema is now defined in `src/db/schema.ts` as TypeScript, with Drizzle generating types from it.
+
+```ts
+export const users = pgTable("users", {
+  id: text("id").primaryKey(),
+  email: text("email").notNull().unique(),
+  createdAt: timestamp("created_at").notNull().defaultNow(),
+});
+export type User = typeof users.$inferSelect;
+```
+
+Queries now reference the schema via the type-safe API:
+
+```ts
+// ✓ Type-safe
+const user = await db.select().from(users).where(eq(users.id, id));
+
+// ✗ This now fails at compile time if columns don't match the schema
+// TypeScript error: Property 'created_on' does not exist on 'users'
+const user = await db.select().from(users).where(eq(users.created_on, ...));
+```
+
+## How It Works
+
+1. **Schema Definition** (`src/db/schema.ts`): Single source of truth for table structure
+2. **Type Generation**: Drizzle auto-generates TypeScript types from the schema
+3. **Compile-Time Validation**: TypeScript checks all query references against the schema
+4. **Migration Sync**: When a migration runs (e.g., rename column), update the schema definition
+5. **Build Error**: Any queries still using old column names fail immediately before deployment
+
+## Workflow for Schema Changes
+
+### Before (vulnerable to production bugs)
+```
+1. Write migration: ALTER TABLE users RENAME COLUMN created_at TO created_on
+2. Run migration on prod
+3. Query in code still references created_at
+4. Bug discovered when query runs → page the team → rollback → retry
+```
+
+### After (caught at build time)
+```
+1. Write migration: ALTER TABLE users RENAME COLUMN created_at TO created_on
+2. Update schema.ts: createdAt: timestamp("created_on")
+3. Run `npm run build` → TypeScript error on all stale queries
+4. Fix queries before merge
+5. Deploy with confidence
+```
+
+## Adding New Tables
+
+For the 9 tables landing this quarter:
+
+1. Add table definition to `src/db/schema.ts`
+2. Update/add queries in `src/db/queries.ts`
+3. TypeScript validates immediately — no runtime surprises
+
+Example:
+```ts
+export const posts = pgTable("posts", {
+  id: text("id").primaryKey(),
+  userId: text("user_id").references(() => users.id),
+  title: text("title").notNull(),
+  body: text("body"),
+  publishedAt: timestamp("published_at"),
+});
+export type Post = typeof posts.$inferSelect;
+```
+
+## When Queries Exceed Schema-as-Code
+
+For complex joins or aggregations that don't map cleanly to the query builder:
+
+```ts
+import { sql } from "drizzle-orm";
+
+// Typed raw SQL: returns User[]
+const result = await db.execute<User>(
+  sql`SELECT u.id, u.email, u.created_at FROM users u WHERE u.id = ${id}`
+);
+```
+
+The schema still drives types; raw SQL falls back to explicit typing when needed.
+
+## Trade-offs
+
+| Aspect | Benefit | Trade-off |
+|--------|---------|-----------|
+| **Compile-time safety** | Catches schema drift before deploy | Requires updating schema definition with migrations |
+| **Type inference** | Auto-generated from schema; no manual typing | Tight coupling to Drizzle (but easily swappable) |
+| **Query builder** | Cleaner than SQL strings; easier refactoring | Less direct control than raw SQL for complex queries |
+| **Dev UX** | TypeScript catches errors immediately | Must run `npm run build` in your loop |
+
+## Testing the Safety
+
+Try adding this to `src/db/queries.ts` and run `npm run build`:
+
+```ts
+// This will cause a TypeScript error:
+const broken = await db.select().from(users).where(eq(users.nonexistent_column, "test"));
+```
+
+Expected error:
+```
+Property 'nonexistent_column' does not exist on type 'typeof users'
+```
+
+That's the safety you now have for all queries.
